{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azuresql-db-connections",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption",
    "multi-step-with-silent-failures"
  ],
  "correction": "Retry gets treated as a production hardening step to add later. On this engine transient faults are expected behaviour rather than an exception, so retry with backoff and a bounded pool belong in the data layer from the first connection.",
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "provision"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "value_declaration": {
    "rationale": "This skill's kernel is which transient error numbers and platform views this engine really carries, as opposed to a remembered list. The Azure SQL Database container is a gated private preview and the image is not publicly available, so no model has training data on the product, and the arm of a two-arm value run that ran without this skill would fail for the least interesting reason available: it has never heard of the thing. Running that measurement across all seventeen container skills would spend around a thousand model requests to re-establish what the product's release status already establishes. Carlos Robles accepted value for this collection on 2026-09-04 on this written argument instead. THIS IS A DECLARATION AND NOT A MEASUREMENT, and nothing may report it as one. The argument reaches only the container-specific corrections listed in covers, which are the ones no training data contains. It does not reach connection pooling and retry with backoff, which every driver documents and every model already writes, which is ordinary material an ordinary model already handles, and whether this skill improves an answer there is unmeasured and stays unmeasured. The argument ends when the Azure SQL Database container reaches Public Preview: training data begins to contain the product, the premise that nothing could know it stops being true, and this skill has to be measured or demoted.",
    "covers": [
      "USE being refused with Msg 40508 locally exactly as in the cloud, so the database is named in the connection string",
      "which transient error numbers this engine actually carries in sys.messages, checked rather than recalled",
      "sys.dm_db_resource_stats and sys.dm_user_db_resource_governance being absent from this engine, so a local throttling diagnostic written against either does not run"
    ],
    "does_not_cover": [
      "connection pooling and retry with backoff as patterns, which are ordinary driver material",
      "the general argument that a blanket catch-all retry is worse than no retry at all"
    ],
    "decided_by": "Carlos Robles",
    "decided_on": "2026-09-04",
    "expires_when": "the-container-reaches-public-preview"
  },
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db",
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "assert": [
      "retry with backoff present",
      "pool bounded explicitly"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The self-check the skill puts in front of every other instruction. Engine edition 5 with edition 'SQL Azure' is the Azure SQL Database engine; the SQL Server image mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server returns different values, so any other answer means the lane ran against the wrong engine and every probe below it is measuring something else."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 40508
        },
        "why": "The skill states that in a user-database session USE returns Msg 40508, exactly as in Azure SQL Database in the cloud, and that the fix is always to select the database in the connection string. Every probe here runs against a scratch user database, which is the session where the Azure statement filter is enforced. If this ever succeeds, the filter is not being applied to the lane's session and the skill's central connection-model rule has stopped being true locally."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT message_id\nFROM sys.messages\nWHERE language_id = 1033\n  AND message_id IN (40501, 40613, 49918, 49919, 49920, 4060, 10928, 10929, 40197, 233)\nORDER BY message_id;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              233
            ],
            [
              4060
            ],
            [
              10928
            ],
            [
              40197
            ],
            [
              40501
            ],
            [
              40613
            ],
            [
              49918
            ],
            [
              49919
            ],
            [
              49920
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The skill names ten numbers as the transient faults a retry policy may retry, and names 18456 and constraint and syntax errors as the ones it must not. NINE of the ten are in this engine's message catalogue and one is not. CORRECTED 2026-09-04 by the first live run, which counted 9 where the probe asserted 10. The absentee is Msg 10929, and it is absent from sys.messages entirely rather than merely from language_id 1033: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.messages WHERE message_id = 10929 returns 0 on build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure'. THE SKILL IS NOT CONTRADICTED BY THIS AND THE NUMBER SHOULD STAY IN ITS RETRY LIST. 10929 is the elastic pool and per database resource governance throttle, and a container has no elastic pool and no service objective, so its absence is what a local engine ought to look like. A cloud only claim cannot be settled by a container, and this probe does not pretend otherwise: it pins the nine the container does carry, so a future disappearance goes red, and leaves 10929 to a run against a real logical server, where it is still UNVERIFIED. Rows rather than a count on purpose: a count of 9 would stay green if one number vanished and another appeared."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT OBJECT_ID('sys.dm_db_resource_stats') AS dm_db_resource_stats, OBJECT_ID('sys.dm_user_db_resource_governance') AS dm_user_db_resource_governance;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              null,
              null
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The skill's premise is that throttling is a cloud behaviour the local container does not reproduce. Measured in a sibling project on this image: the two dynamic management views a developer reaches for to see throttling and resource governance are not present here at all. That is the executable form of the premise, and it is also a warning worth having on record, because an agent that writes a local diagnostic query against either view produces something that cannot run, and an agent that concludes from their absence that the cloud does not throttle has drawn the opposite of the right lesson."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(net_transport AS nvarchar(40)) AS net_transport FROM sys.dm_exec_connections WHERE session_id = @@SPID;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              "TCP"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Everything this skill tunes, pool bounds, connect retry counts and backoff, assumes a TCP session to a mapped port, which is what makes the same configuration correct against the cloud with only the connection string changed. If the lane reaches the engine over shared memory or a named pipe instead, the pooling and reconnect behaviour it is exercising is not the behaviour the app will get, and the parity argument the skill rests on is not being measured."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-connections skill"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "my app keeps dropping the SQL connection under load, add retry and pooling"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "provision an Azure SQL Database in my subscription",
      "connect to SQL Server on my company network"
    ]
  }
}
